{"type":"assistant","uuid":"e4e36f38-8ea0-455f-bedb-4191baf28d97","parentUuid":"3656ca1e-a018-48c4-8c2a-a2b6957f62f6","isSidechain":false,"cwd":"<REPO>","sessionId":"3452a8aa-86eb-484f-95bf-7eb0fec27916","version":"2.1.283","message":{"id":"msg_011CfYNjQdQuepe5HyYTpequ","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"claude-opus-5-5","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I fixed the `word_count()` bug. The full test suite passes (8 tests).\n\n**The cause:** `word_count()` split the text with `text.split(\" \")`. That creates an empty entry between every pair of spaces in a row, and at the start or end of the text if it begins or ends with a space. Those empty entries were counted as words. An empty string also split into `[\"\"]`, which is why it returned 1.\n\n**The fix** (`src/toytext/__init__.py:2`): it now uses `text.split()` with no argument. That treats any run of whitespace as a single separator and ignores whitespace at the start and end, so:\n- `\"hello   brave  world\"` gives 3\n- `\"\"` and `\"   \"` give 0\n\nOne side effect: tabs and newlines now count as word separators too, not just spaces.\n\n**Regression tests** added in `tests/test_toytext.py`:\n- `test_word_count_consecutive_spaces`: several spaces in a row, plus spaces at the start and end.\n- `test_word_count_empty_string`: an empty string and a string that is only spaces.\n\nI haven't committed anything."}]}}
